<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imam</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An imam is a Muslim religious leader, ranging from the leader of congregational prayer to, in Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, the divinely guided head of the community. The Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> claimed to be imams in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> sense. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Imam Ahmad</a> ibn Ibrahim held the title while leading the wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imam.jpg" alt="Imam" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nasreddine Dinet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imam_presidant_la_priere.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Government thereafter became effectively theocratic: Ahmad ruled as <b>Imam</b> rather than sultan, drawing legitimacy from his position as leader of the jihad against Christian Ethiopia, while the Walashma sultan remained a nominal sovereign.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Somali traditions, the Ajuran was ruled by <b>imams</b> of the Garen lineage, whose claim to sacred descent gave them a religious authority that set them above ordinary clan leaders.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ajuran-sultanate">Ajuran Sultanate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nizari Ismailis who broke away after the 1094 succession crisis gave rise to the <b>imams</b> known today as the Aga Khans, a lineage tracing back to the Fatimid missionary network.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Idris I left no male heir when an Abbasid agent poisoned him in 791, but his wife Lalla Kanza bore his only son shortly after his death; the boy, Idris II, was proclaimed <b>imam</b> by the Awraba at the age of eleven.</blockquote><a href="/empire/idrisid-dynasty">Idrisid dynasty · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Hulagu destroyed the Nizari Ismaili state in 1256, securing the surrender of its <b>imam</b> and, after the Siege of Alamut, the fortress itself, then advanced into Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Islam reached the court under Yaji (1349–1385), when Wangara Muslims from the Mali Empire arrived and supplied an <b>imam</b> and a judge, but it spread gradually, and many rural Hausa, the Maguzawa, kept the old religion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-kano">Kingdom of Kano · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
